Dafny now rejects storing, in a field or an array element, a value that holds a function that reads the heap inside a datatype. Before, the check looked for such a function (`~>`) only in the type of the stored value and its type arguments. A field of type `W`, with `datatype W = W(f: () ~> bool)`, could hold a function that reads that field and applies what it finds. Applying a function value gets no termination check, so such a function proved `false`. The check now also looks inside the constructor arguments of inductive and coinductive datatypes, and inside the base types of newtypes and subset types, with the type arguments substituted. The hidden option `--legacy-reading-functions-in-datatypes` restores the old, unsound check, for comparison and migration only.
